Ladybird now keeps IndexedDB databases in Hub, so every agent of a
browsing session sees the same ones, whichever process it runs in,
as in the other engines: Blink holds them in its browser process
(content/browser/indexed_db/instance’s BucketContext), WebKit in
its network process (IDBServer), and Gecko in its parent process
(dom/indexedDB/ActorsParent.cpp). In Chrome 158, a page’s storage
survived the browsing-context-group switch that the two Cross-Origin-
Opener-Policy values of web.whatsapp.com cause, served from a local test
server. Before this change, Ladybird lost the page’s database.

A transaction that a page creates just before it closes the connection
commits on its own once the task ends, in Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and
WebKit 26.5, and now in Ladybird too.
